From: MeowArchMobile
Subject: [PATCH] adb_listeners.cpp: disable Nagle on accepted sockets

Upstream turns TCP_NODELAY on for every socket it creates itself — the
client's side in socket_spec.cpp's socket_spec_connect(), adbd's in
daemon/transport_socket_server.cpp, adb_wifi.cpp — but not for the
sockets it ACCEPTS from clients, in ss_listener_event_func() and
listener_event_func(). Those are exactly the sockets the adb server
writes many small replies to.

With Nagle left on, a reply that follows a still-unacknowledged reply
waits for a delayed ACK from a peer that is itself blocked writing.
Measured on loopback with 1000 one-file directories, one LIST per
directory: 6-12 ms per directory instead of ~0.05 ms, i.e. the entire
cost of "pull: building file list..." was this interaction rather than
any syscall or disk work. Disabling it makes a recursive pull's listing
phase roughly three orders of magnitude cheaper, and it is what lets the
client-side request batching in file_sync_client.cpp pay off (without
it, batching actually made things WORSE at small windows: 4.5 s at
window 8 versus 0.09 s at window 1).

A no-op on unix-domain sockets, so local-only setups are unaffected.

diff --git a/adb_listeners.cpp b/adb_listeners.cpp
index c24016f..19fc810 100644
--- a/adb_listeners.cpp
+++ b/adb_listeners.cpp
@@ -82,6 +82,16 @@ static void ss_listener_event_func(int _fd, unsigned ev, void *_l) {
         unique_fd fd(adb_socket_accept(_fd, nullptr, nullptr));
         if (fd < 0) return;
 
+        // MeowArchMobile: upstream disables Nagle on every other socket it
+        // creates — the client's side in socket_spec_connect(), adbd's in
+        // transport_socket_server.cpp — but not on the sockets it accepts from
+        // clients, which are exactly the ones it writes many small replies to.
+        // With Nagle on here, a reply that follows a still-unacknowledged reply
+        // waits for a delayed ACK, which measured ~12 ms per directory listing
+        // and is what made "pull: building file list..." scale with the number
+        // of directories instead of their bytes. A no-op on unix sockets.
+        disable_tcp_nagle(fd.get());
+
         int rcv_buf_size = CHUNK_SIZE;
         adb_setsockopt(fd.get(), SOL_SOCKET, SO_RCVBUF, &rcv_buf_size, sizeof(rcv_buf_size));
 
@@ -104,6 +114,9 @@ static void listener_event_func(int _fd, unsigned ev, void* _l)
             return;
         }
 
+        // See ss_listener_event_func: same omission, same fix.
+        disable_tcp_nagle(fd.get());
+
         asocket* s = create_local_socket(std::move(fd));
         if (s) {
             s->transport = listener->transport;
